# Line endings are LF in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# `.editorconfig` already requires LF, and this app runs inside a Linux container,
# so a CRLF working copy would be a difference that shows up in every diff and in
# nothing else. Git for Windows turns on `core.autocrlf` by default; this file
# overrides that for every clone rather than relying on a local setting.
* text=auto eol=lf

# A workbook is a ZIP archive, and `text=auto` only declines to touch it because the bytes
# happen to contain a NUL. Saying so outright is what keeps a future `eol` rule, a filter or a
# different Git version from rewriting the one file the spreadsheet tests read
# (`smartprocess_logistics/tests/fixtures/synthetic-workbook.xlsx`) — a rewrite of a ZIP is a
# workbook openpyxl refuses, and the test that proves the intake's reading order would fail with
# an error about the fixture rather than about the order.
*.xlsx binary

# Files that belong to this machine's way of working rather than to the app. `export-ignore`
# keeps them out of `git archive` — and so out of the tarball or ZIP a release page offers —
# while leaving them in the working tree and in history, where they are used. A clone still
# sees them: taking them out of the repository is a history decision, not an attribute.
CLAUDE.md export-ignore
.claude/ export-ignore
docs/test-manifest.md export-ignore
